WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.087 --> 00:00:02.996
[MUSIC]

00:00:02.996 --> 00:00:07.985
We all have many decisions to make, some
are easy and straightforward, while others

00:00:07.985 --> 00:00:12.369
can be tricky in terms of appropriateness,
effectiveness, and ethics.

00:00:12.369 --> 00:00:16.691
Today I look forward to digging in and
exploring ethics in our personal and

00:00:16.691 --> 00:00:18.027
professional lives.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:19.281
I'm Matt Abrahams, and

00:00:19.281 --> 00:00:23.113
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:23.113 --> 00:00:27.760
Welcome to Think Fast,
Talk Smart, the podcast.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:32.740
I am super excited to be joined
by Neil Malhotra and Ken Schatz.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.178
Neil is the Edith M Cornell professor
of political economy at the GSB.

00:00:37.178 --> 00:00:41.016
He also serves as the director of
the Center for Social Innovation.

00:00:41.016 --> 00:00:42.602
Ken is the David S and and

00:00:42.602 --> 00:00:47.210
M Barlow professor of political
economy also at the GSB.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:51.200
Together Neal and Ken can teach
a core course on business ethics.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.495
And recently they published a new
book together called Leading with

00:00:55.495 --> 00:01:00.910
Values Strategies for Making Ethical
Decisions in Business and Life.

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:02.000
Thanks for being here.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.860
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Matt, so great to be here.

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:04.870
&gt;&gt; Thanks so much, Matt.

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:06.750
&gt;&gt; All right, let's get going.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.736
Neil, you and Ken teach business ethics,
can you share with us how you two think

00:01:11.736 --> 00:01:15.990
about ethics and the role they play or
should play in business?

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.148
&gt;&gt; Sure, that's a great question.

00:01:17.148 --> 00:01:22.498
I would say that the concept of the role
in business and society has changed

00:01:22.498 --> 00:01:27.770
a lot in the last 30 years and
is going through a sea change as we speak.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.626
And I think that probably
wouldn't be new to your audience,

00:01:30.626 --> 00:01:34.280
given all of the social issues
that businesses are involved in.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:39.116
And I would say in the 1980s, there was
a big emphasis on shareholder value,

00:01:39.116 --> 00:01:43.312
that the primary ethical and
moral responsibility of a business was to

00:01:43.312 --> 00:01:47.741
maximize value for its shareholders,
who are the owners of the company.

00:01:47.741 --> 00:01:52.242
And I think kind of increasingly we've
moved to more of a stakeholder model,

00:01:52.242 --> 00:01:56.762
where businesses have to be aware of
various stakeholder groups in society.

00:01:56.762 --> 00:02:00.823
And this is what kind of motivated us
to write the book which is, how do you

00:02:00.823 --> 00:02:05.685
actually rigorously and analytically think
about your role as a business leader given

00:02:05.685 --> 00:02:09.415
all of these new challenges and
responsibilities you might have,

00:02:09.415 --> 00:02:13.630
where various groups sort of demand
that businesses act ethically?

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:18.105
And we draw on a lot of intellectual
traditions ranging from psychology to

00:02:18.105 --> 00:02:21.872
normative philosophy,
to economics, political science.

00:02:21.872 --> 00:02:26.411
And kind of gather all those insights so
the readers can have really tangible,

00:02:26.411 --> 00:02:30.886
actionable recommendations on how to
lead organizations in the challenging

00:02:30.886 --> 00:02:33.020
environment we currently face.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.772
&gt;&gt; I really appreciated in your book,
how you did that,

00:02:35.772 --> 00:02:39.946
where you draw on all these different ways
of thinking and ways of studying this.

00:02:39.946 --> 00:02:43.376
And then the very specific advice and
guidance you give is very helpful and

00:02:43.376 --> 00:02:46.439
I love that you have some activities for
people to think through.

00:02:46.439 --> 00:02:49.808
And I can see how it would help our
students as they prepare to enter

00:02:49.808 --> 00:02:52.230
the business world and act ethically.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:56.938
Ken, you write that effective
leaders not only know their values,

00:02:56.938 --> 00:03:02.310
but can manage the diversity of
values within their organizations.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.506
How do you recommend that people
identify their core values and

00:03:05.506 --> 00:03:09.290
determine those of others on their team or
in their company?

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:13.956
&gt;&gt; I think the first thing I would
say is what not to do is to look at

00:03:13.956 --> 00:03:18.730
the company's public statements
of its mission and values.

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:23.743
I think for some companies, those are very
authentic to what the company is about and

00:03:23.743 --> 00:03:26.370
what motivates people within the company.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:28.157
But when I look at those statements,

00:03:28.157 --> 00:03:32.010
there's often a lot of similarity
across companies in those statements.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.715
And I don't think that really reflects
the differences across companies and

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.050
amongst individuals within companies.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.828
So I would say the first thing this
requires, and this is implicit in the idea

00:03:42.828 --> 00:03:47.829
of managing a diversity of values, but
I think it's a mistake that some leaders

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:53.000
is to, don't assume that the people in
your organization share your own values.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.622
Some of the time they do,
some of the time they don't,

00:03:55.622 --> 00:03:58.814
some of the time some of them do,
but there's a bunch who don't and

00:03:58.814 --> 00:04:01.273
those are the most difficult
sorts of situations.

00:04:01.273 --> 00:04:06.108
We have this exercise, actually the
concluding chapter of the book is to have,

00:04:06.108 --> 00:04:10.146
in which we do in our MBA class is to
have people sit down and think and

00:04:10.146 --> 00:04:12.111
write up their own core values.

00:04:12.111 --> 00:04:16.056
And drawing on stuff from the book but
also religious traditions,

00:04:16.056 --> 00:04:20.848
cultural traditions, personal history
often really plays an important role in

00:04:20.848 --> 00:04:22.761
driving people's own values.

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:26.533
And I think as a leader, that's
something that one can do for oneself.

00:04:26.533 --> 00:04:30.988
One can also, you can imagine
doing this as a team exercise.

00:04:30.988 --> 00:04:34.437
But the problem with that is some of
the time people feel they have to present

00:04:34.437 --> 00:04:37.530
themselves a certain way in the work
context and things like that.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:43.890
So it can be really tricky to figure out
the points where, disagree with oneself.

00:04:43.890 --> 00:04:49.186
And I think that's why one of our big
important takeaways from the book is like,

00:04:49.186 --> 00:04:54.327
well set up organizations allow for
dissent in disagreement about values and

00:04:54.327 --> 00:04:58.537
to make it possible feasible
encouraged for people to do that.

00:04:58.537 --> 00:05:02.329
That can be living with some conflict
that can be uncomfortable for people.

00:05:02.329 --> 00:05:07.623
&gt;&gt; I really appreciate that last point
about how it can be uncomfortable but it

00:05:07.623 --> 00:05:13.880
allows for a more free organization where
people can share their different opinions.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.961
We have on a number of occasions
on this podcast talked

00:05:16.961 --> 00:05:21.960
about the notion of psychological safety,
about self reflection on your position and

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:22.996
your attitudes.

00:05:22.996 --> 00:05:26.559
And to that you had this list of values,
and I think that's really important.

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:30.040
And taking the time to just reflect
on your values and write them down,

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.462
I think can be very helpful.

00:05:31.462 --> 00:05:34.224
And finding ways to encourage
your colleagues and

00:05:34.224 --> 00:05:38.301
others can also help you set up an
environment where people really can feel

00:05:38.301 --> 00:05:42.333
that it's okay to share their point
of view and to disagree with others.

00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:43.589
So thank you for that.

00:05:43.589 --> 00:05:48.523
Neil, you and Ken make the case that
our values lead to the moral choices we

00:05:48.523 --> 00:05:51.159
make and motivate the actions we take.

00:05:51.159 --> 00:05:55.094
What thoughts do you have about
how we can assess our actions?

00:05:55.094 --> 00:05:58.949
In your book you discuss different
tests people can use and deploy.

00:05:58.949 --> 00:06:02.459
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so this kind of ties to
something you mentioned earlier,

00:06:02.459 --> 00:06:06.749
which is kind of one thing we tried to do
in the book is take abstract concepts and

00:06:06.749 --> 00:06:09.687
convert them into tangible tests and
evaluate them.

00:06:09.687 --> 00:06:13.852
So we have one chapter where we
talk about where organizations and

00:06:13.852 --> 00:06:16.361
leaders can maybe go on the wrong path.

00:06:16.361 --> 00:06:16.891
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:06:16.891 --> 00:06:20.639
&gt;&gt; And so we kind of lay out various
tests that can prevent people

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:25.431
from veering in the wrong decision that
are inconsistent with their values.

00:06:25.431 --> 00:06:27.781
For example,
we talk about bright line rules,

00:06:27.781 --> 00:06:29.677
this is an approach many people take.

00:06:29.677 --> 00:06:32.275
An example we give is Clayton Christensen,

00:06:32.275 --> 00:06:36.007
the famous management scholar
who passed away a few years ago.

00:06:36.007 --> 00:06:41.107
He had a Mormon upbringing with the LDS
and a bit, he had these bright line rules,

00:06:41.107 --> 00:06:44.784
for example, that he was going to
use Sunday as a day of rest.

00:06:44.784 --> 00:06:49.605
And he tells an anecdote about how he
cost his basketball team the championship

00:06:49.605 --> 00:06:52.140
because he didn't play on that day.

00:06:52.140 --> 00:06:54.603
But the whole point he
was trying to say is,

00:06:54.603 --> 00:06:59.405
is that if you violate your bright line
rules then those rules become meaningless.

00:06:59.405 --> 00:07:02.961
So as we kind of discussed
that's a really valuable and

00:07:02.961 --> 00:07:07.754
very clear way to design a test, but
there's many situations in a complex

00:07:07.754 --> 00:07:13.119
business environment where you can't
predict exactly what's going to happen.

00:07:13.119 --> 00:07:16.803
So there's not going to be bright
line rules you can list in advance.

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:18.820
So then we talked about other tests.

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:23.267
So I think a very common one business
leaders talk about is the New York Times

00:07:23.267 --> 00:07:27.856
test or The Wall Street Journal test,
which is always think the New York Times

00:07:27.856 --> 00:07:31.480
was writing about what you did,
would you do the same thing?

00:07:31.480 --> 00:07:33.750
And as we explain,
there's some flaws with this test.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:37.216
And one of the flaws is, is that the New
York Times doesn't really write about very

00:07:37.216 --> 00:07:39.540
much stuff that's going
on in the [LAUGH] world.

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:43.710
So most of the decisions we face
are not fair in those level decisions.

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:48.707
They are things that the media are not
going to cover but actually, have a lot of

00:07:48.707 --> 00:07:54.251
implications on organizations, how we lead
and how people perceive our leadership.

00:07:54.251 --> 00:07:56.926
So we talked about actually
a third test which is.

00:07:56.926 --> 00:08:01.439
The friends and family test, would you
be comfortable telling your friends and

00:08:01.439 --> 00:08:03.998
family about your actions and
decisions, or

00:08:03.998 --> 00:08:07.519
are you actually hiding things
from your friends and family?

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:10.446
And this is actually something
that's very practical and

00:08:10.446 --> 00:08:12.665
realistic because it
comes up all the time.

00:08:12.665 --> 00:08:15.863
You're really not going to be on
the phone with a New York Times reporter,

00:08:15.863 --> 00:08:18.846
but you will be talking to your spouse or
your loved ones at night and

00:08:18.846 --> 00:08:20.305
telling them how your day went.

00:08:20.305 --> 00:08:24.530
And I think it's actually a very telling
test where you're withholding information

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:28.240
because you think that there's
inconsistent with your value structure.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.798
Now, we talk about
limitations of that test too.

00:08:30.798 --> 00:08:35.461
For example, sometimes our friends and
family ate in a bed, us, but nonetheless,

00:08:35.461 --> 00:08:39.404
I think we tried to kind of explain
different tests and the strengths and

00:08:39.404 --> 00:08:41.067
limitations of each of them.

00:08:41.067 --> 00:08:44.687
&gt;&gt; So I was smiling as you were talking
about the friends and family tests,

00:08:44.687 --> 00:08:48.384
because with my two teenage boys,
we have what we call the grandma test.

00:08:48.384 --> 00:08:51.968
What would you think if grandma found
out about what you've said or did, and

00:08:51.968 --> 00:08:53.153
that resonated with me.

00:08:53.153 --> 00:08:57.182
It sounds to me though that thinking
through how you will assess or

00:08:57.182 --> 00:08:59.976
test your actions is
really important to do.

00:08:59.976 --> 00:09:02.811
And to have a shared agreement,
not just with yourself, but

00:09:02.811 --> 00:09:04.544
others on your team would also help.

00:09:04.544 --> 00:09:09.665
So, I encourage everybody to think about
how will you assess those decisions and

00:09:09.665 --> 00:09:11.183
actions that you take.

00:09:11.183 --> 00:09:15.565
Can you talk about how situational
social environments and

00:09:15.565 --> 00:09:18.898
norms can also influence
our moral actions?

00:09:18.898 --> 00:09:23.714
What best practices can you suggest
to help us not only understand these

00:09:23.714 --> 00:09:27.815
external influences but
work to align them with our values?

00:09:27.815 --> 00:09:32.357
&gt;&gt; So, I like how you phrase that, Matt,
that is not treating this external

00:09:32.357 --> 00:09:37.047
situation is something that's just
kind of fixed, and I gotta live with it,

00:09:37.047 --> 00:09:41.324
recognizing that the situations we
put ourselves into are a choice.

00:09:41.324 --> 00:09:42.209
And actually,

00:09:42.209 --> 00:09:47.047
that's often the most important choice we
make that's going to determine what we do.

00:09:47.047 --> 00:09:51.610
So I think there's this tendency in
Western cultures, specifically or

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.078
especially, and the American West and

00:09:54.078 --> 00:09:57.539
Silicon Valley to valorize
their heroic individual.

00:09:57.539 --> 00:09:59.815
And yeah, the thing is with
the psychologists have shown,

00:09:59.815 --> 00:10:02.881
I'm actually not a psychologist scholar,
I'm a political economy scholar.

00:10:02.881 --> 00:10:06.182
But I've looked at what
the lessons of social psychology,

00:10:06.182 --> 00:10:10.749
they've shown pretty well that we are not
heroic individuals, we just aren't.

00:10:10.749 --> 00:10:13.224
It'd be great if we were, but we aren't.

00:10:13.224 --> 00:10:16.920
I mean, a good thing though is
that we are capable of being

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:20.164
great if we put ourselves
in the right situation.

00:10:20.164 --> 00:10:24.743
So I think the first lesson is
not assuming I will do the right

00:10:24.743 --> 00:10:27.594
thing no matter the circumstances.

00:10:27.594 --> 00:10:32.043
I think planning ahead for taking
the circumstances that I put myself into

00:10:32.043 --> 00:10:36.003
will shape what I will do and
maybe even shape the person I become.

00:10:36.003 --> 00:10:40.083
And I think this is one of
the most important lessons and

00:10:40.083 --> 00:10:45.051
things for people to think about
when thinking about jobs to take or

00:10:45.051 --> 00:10:47.549
lines of professions to go into.

00:10:47.549 --> 00:10:49.747
All sorts of career planning things,

00:10:49.747 --> 00:10:53.743
is like I'm going to be part of this
organization in this situation.

00:10:53.743 --> 00:10:57.510
Do I want to be like the people
in this organization?

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.553
Is that what I aspire to for myself?

00:11:00.553 --> 00:11:04.388
And I think that's a really foundational,
like planning thing.

00:11:04.388 --> 00:11:08.312
And then the other part of this is no
matter what situations we put ourselves

00:11:08.312 --> 00:11:12.047
into, some of the times stuff is going to
come up that pushes us to do things

00:11:12.047 --> 00:11:14.374
in ways that are out of
step with our values.

00:11:14.374 --> 00:11:19.462
And I think the biggest lesson there
is to just try to find a way to

00:11:19.462 --> 00:11:24.935
get oneself out of the situation,
I mean, literally physically,

00:11:24.935 --> 00:11:29.943
I mean, immediate physical context
has a lot of effect on us.

00:11:29.943 --> 00:11:33.597
But also emotionally, this sort of goes
back to the friends and family test.

00:11:33.597 --> 00:11:38.581
I think it's good to bounce things off
of those who were close to those who

00:11:38.581 --> 00:11:39.325
we trust.

00:11:39.325 --> 00:11:44.290
Those who we trust to be willing to
criticize us because they value us and

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:48.288
they love us,
I think that's a really important thing.

00:11:48.288 --> 00:11:50.414
So I think that's kind of
a long winded answer, but

00:11:50.414 --> 00:11:54.340
I think the question you asked is probably
more important than the answer I gave.

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:58.170
&gt;&gt; That question was primed by
a discussion we had on a previous podcast

00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:00.859
episode where I interviewed
Philip Zimbardo,

00:12:00.859 --> 00:12:04.754
one of the most famous social
psychologists who has dedicated a lot of

00:12:04.754 --> 00:12:08.276
his career to studying how
the situation influences people.

00:12:08.276 --> 00:12:12.492
So, that was fresh in my mind as I was
reading that chapter of your book.

00:12:12.492 --> 00:12:16.864
Though, the other thing you mentioned,
Ken, that I think is really critical is

00:12:16.864 --> 00:12:21.575
that you have to have trusted others that
can give you good, clear, honest feedback.

00:12:21.575 --> 00:12:24.377
And that can be hard sometimes
in a business setting,

00:12:24.377 --> 00:12:26.371
because people don't want to offend or

00:12:26.371 --> 00:12:30.626
they're afraid that there might be
political or hierarchical ramifications.

00:12:30.626 --> 00:12:33.478
So that's important to
find those trusted others.

00:12:33.478 --> 00:12:37.548
I love that you too dedicate time
in your business ethics class and

00:12:37.548 --> 00:12:41.038
in your book to discuss strategies for
communication.

00:12:41.038 --> 00:12:43.733
I had a big smile on my
face as I was reading that.

00:12:43.733 --> 00:12:48.756
Neil, can you share the pointers you
provide around understanding both

00:12:48.756 --> 00:12:53.625
your audience and yourself, and
framing messages to align morally?

00:12:53.625 --> 00:12:55.455
&gt;&gt; Sure, and
this kind of ties to what you just said.

00:12:55.455 --> 00:13:00.167
People are oftentimes worried in these
polarized times that there's just

00:13:00.167 --> 00:13:04.515
a lot of disagreement, conflict,
it's hard to reach resolution.

00:13:04.515 --> 00:13:05.570
So in the book,

00:13:05.570 --> 00:13:11.192
we try to bridge literature on moral
philosophy with communication strategies.

00:13:11.192 --> 00:13:15.803
And specifically, we're very influenced by
what's called moral foundations theory.

00:13:15.803 --> 00:13:20.314
Which is the argument that everybody
has sort of these deep seated moral

00:13:20.314 --> 00:13:24.524
intuitions and foundations,
which drive how they see the world,

00:13:24.524 --> 00:13:28.154
their kind of senses,
just like we have touch and taste.

00:13:28.154 --> 00:13:31.274
We have kind of moral senses
that guide how we see it.

00:13:31.274 --> 00:13:35.822
And once you realize this, you realize
that when we disagree with someone,

00:13:35.822 --> 00:13:40.885
it's oftentimes that they are coming and
using a different moral sense than we are.

00:13:40.885 --> 00:13:45.362
So an example is like debates over
COVID lockdowns in the workplace or

00:13:45.362 --> 00:13:46.682
vaccine mandates.

00:13:46.682 --> 00:13:50.729
Some people, the moral sense they
really are attuned to is harm, so

00:13:50.729 --> 00:13:54.712
anything they can do to reduce harm
to other people would be great.

00:13:54.712 --> 00:13:57.864
Other people are motivated
by a moral sense of liberty.

00:13:57.864 --> 00:14:01.271
Which is that people should have
the freedom to do what they want, and

00:14:01.271 --> 00:14:04.046
kind of restricting their
freedom is a negative thing.

00:14:04.046 --> 00:14:08.542
So if, for example, you want to implement
a tough COVID policy, whether it's like

00:14:08.542 --> 00:14:13.058
a vaccine mandate or something like that,
and you come from the harm perspective.

00:14:13.058 --> 00:14:17.646
It's a really ineffective strategy
to convince people from the liberty

00:14:17.646 --> 00:14:21.795
perspective using language and
argument centered around harm.

00:14:21.795 --> 00:14:24.928
So that's telling a story
how you've been told it,

00:14:24.928 --> 00:14:28.784
not looking from their perspectives and
the stories they tell.

00:14:28.784 --> 00:14:33.139
The most effective thing you can do is to
understand the other person's story and

00:14:33.139 --> 00:14:35.712
frame the language and
arguments around them.

00:14:35.712 --> 00:14:39.115
So to argue that, hey,
if you really care about liberty,

00:14:39.115 --> 00:14:42.450
we actually need these vaccines
because this will open up

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:46.349
a flourishing economy that will
allow us to do everything we want.

00:14:46.349 --> 00:14:49.118
And I think kind of this
is what framing is about,

00:14:49.118 --> 00:14:51.627
is fundamentally about being empathetic.

00:14:51.627 --> 00:14:54.780
And there's been a lot of
research done on perspective,

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:57.085
taking in how to change people's minds.

00:14:57.085 --> 00:15:00.993
The results are just very clear, which is
the worst thing you can do is show up to

00:15:00.993 --> 00:15:04.324
someone's house and
explain to them why they're morally wrong.

00:15:04.324 --> 00:15:08.502
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That tends to not work very well.

00:15:08.502 --> 00:15:12.618
Whereas, when they've tried to change
people's minds on gay marriage or

00:15:12.618 --> 00:15:16.730
transgender rights, they start by
listening, by saying, well, tell me

00:15:16.730 --> 00:15:20.544
a story of when you felt discriminated
against or you felt excluded.

00:15:20.544 --> 00:15:23.810
And then they get people talking
about themselves, and they say,

00:15:23.810 --> 00:15:27.914
well, this is what happened to this other
person, and they felt a lot like you did.

00:15:27.914 --> 00:15:30.935
And so,
coming at it from a non-judgmental view,

00:15:30.935 --> 00:15:34.884
listening rather than lecturing,
it tends to be most effective.

00:15:34.884 --> 00:15:38.026
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you took me back to
our thanksgiving table where we

00:15:38.026 --> 00:15:41.309
had lots of people being told
that they were morally wrong.

00:15:41.309 --> 00:15:44.144
[LAUGH] So I appreciate that.

00:15:44.144 --> 00:15:47.634
This notion of moral
sense is really valuable.

00:15:47.634 --> 00:15:51.463
We talk a lot about understanding your
audience, taking their perspective,

00:15:51.463 --> 00:15:55.920
empathy, Moral sense is yet another one of
the things to consider when we talk about

00:15:55.920 --> 00:15:57.889
attitudes, resistance points, but

00:15:57.889 --> 00:16:02.275
also somebody's moral perspective and how
you can adjust and adapt your messages.

00:16:02.275 --> 00:16:05.174
So it's not just that you
have to appreciate it,

00:16:05.174 --> 00:16:09.923
you then have to frame your message in
alignment with somebody's moral sense.

00:16:09.923 --> 00:16:11.685
And thank you for that, and

00:16:11.685 --> 00:16:15.597
the examples you used were very
helpful in explaining that.

00:16:15.597 --> 00:16:16.443
Before we end,

00:16:16.443 --> 00:16:20.562
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I asked everyone who joins me.

00:16:20.562 --> 00:16:22.282
Are you gentlemen up for that?

00:16:22.282 --> 00:16:23.158
&gt;&gt; Sure, of course.

00:16:23.158 --> 00:16:25.465
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I guess we can't
duck it if you ask everyone.

00:16:25.465 --> 00:16:26.992
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's true.
You would definitely stand out.

00:16:26.992 --> 00:16:29.253
So Ken, we're going to start with you.

00:16:29.253 --> 00:16:33.662
If you were to capture the best
communication advice you have

00:16:33.662 --> 00:16:38.331
ever received as a five to seven
word presentation slide title,

00:16:38.331 --> 00:16:39.808
what would that be?

00:16:39.808 --> 00:16:43.307
&gt;&gt; Your audience is not yourself.

00:16:43.307 --> 00:16:49.025
And to really internalize that, when
communicating, I think is hard, because we

00:16:49.025 --> 00:16:54.350
naturally kind of, talk to ourselves and
I play up things that we believe it.

00:16:54.350 --> 00:16:58.783
&gt;&gt; I like how you captured it I often say
in my strategic communication classes that

00:16:58.783 --> 00:17:02.436
the biggest mistake people make
is we start from the wrong place.

00:17:02.436 --> 00:17:08.220
We start from saying here's what I want to
say and that puts us on the wrong path.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:13.185
You're title of your slide really
emphasizes that, thank you.

00:17:13.185 --> 00:17:14.532
Now for both of you,

00:17:14.532 --> 00:17:19.138
I'd like you to answer who is
a communicator that you admire and why?

00:17:19.138 --> 00:17:21.051
And Neil, why don't you go first?

00:17:21.051 --> 00:17:24.939
&gt;&gt; I would say the late Steve Jobs is
someone I admired might admit, even though

00:17:24.939 --> 00:17:29.269
people kind of disagreed with maybe his
personality or things about his decisions.

00:17:29.269 --> 00:17:34.273
One reason I think he was
an effective communicator is that he

00:17:34.273 --> 00:17:39.766
knew his core values and
his company's core values really well and

00:17:39.766 --> 00:17:42.726
can motivate people based on them.

00:17:42.726 --> 00:17:46.049
The book has a link to his YouTube
video when he came back to

00:17:46.049 --> 00:17:48.277
Apple after he was originally fired.

00:17:48.277 --> 00:17:52.921
And a lot of people don't remember
because it's the most valuable company in

00:17:52.921 --> 00:17:56.361
the US now but it was a very
crappy company for a long time.

00:17:56.361 --> 00:17:59.876
After Jobs left, the company was
basically going down the toilet and

00:17:59.876 --> 00:18:03.935
this was also during the.com boom kind of
before it where a lot of people had a lot

00:18:03.935 --> 00:18:06.251
of good job offers and
could have left Apple.

00:18:06.251 --> 00:18:10.191
And I really encourage people to watch
this video to kind of see what great

00:18:10.191 --> 00:18:12.913
communication around values and
core values is.

00:18:12.913 --> 00:18:15.995
And he explained what is
the core value of Apple.

00:18:15.995 --> 00:18:21.251
We are not in the business of making
boxes to help people get their work done.

00:18:21.251 --> 00:18:26.183
We are in the business of enabling
disruptors to change the world.

00:18:26.183 --> 00:18:31.122
And he said basically encapsulated
Apple's core value is this which is

00:18:31.122 --> 00:18:35.320
Martin Luther King and
Amelia Earhart never had a computer,

00:18:35.320 --> 00:18:39.206
but if they had one,
you know they would have used a Mac.

00:18:39.206 --> 00:18:41.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's just brilliant when you really

00:18:41.480 --> 00:18:42.221
think about it.

00:18:42.221 --> 00:18:46.227
And it really explains what Apple's
customer base likes to believe about

00:18:46.227 --> 00:18:50.638
themselves and what the employees in
Apple like to believe about themselves..

00:18:50.638 --> 00:18:55.536
And so I think just to kind of sum
opposite what he wasn't good communicator

00:18:55.536 --> 00:18:57.260
because he was a good speaker.

00:18:57.260 --> 00:19:00.820
He was a good communicator because
he understand his values or

00:19:00.820 --> 00:19:05.070
was able to explain the very clearly
we'd like a simple phrase like that.

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:05.696
&gt;&gt; I love it.
And

00:19:05.696 --> 00:19:09.388
we also use that speech in the strategic
communication class I teach, so

00:19:09.388 --> 00:19:13.036
I'm very familiar with it, and
I do encourage everybody to look at it.

00:19:13.036 --> 00:19:16.818
So Ken, who is a communicator
that you admire and why?

00:19:16.818 --> 00:19:23.006
&gt;&gt; The communicators who I really admire
are great elementary school teachers.

00:19:23.006 --> 00:19:23.554
&gt;&gt; Hah.

00:19:23.554 --> 00:19:27.383
&gt;&gt; And it's because they do things I
mean they have a challenging audience to

00:19:27.383 --> 00:19:28.010
work with.

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.750
Like we think we have a challenging
audience to teaching MBAs.

00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:31.621
&gt;&gt; It is nothing.

00:19:31.621 --> 00:19:36.086
&gt;&gt; It is nothing compared to what an
elementary school teacher is dealing with.

00:19:36.086 --> 00:19:40.603
A great elementary school teacher,
they know their audience.

00:19:40.603 --> 00:19:42.749
They know how to treat them respectfully,
but

00:19:42.749 --> 00:19:45.673
they also know what they know
when the audience doesn't know.

00:19:45.673 --> 00:19:50.403
So they know how to simplify and
will ruthlessly simplify their

00:19:50.403 --> 00:19:55.313
core message to get across the stuff
the audience really needs and

00:19:55.313 --> 00:19:59.523
to tell this as a story and
make it vivid to the students.

00:19:59.523 --> 00:20:03.432
And I'm just in awe of
the skill it takes to do this.

00:20:03.432 --> 00:20:05.731
I mean, this is an incredible craft.

00:20:05.731 --> 00:20:07.225
I'm just blown away.

00:20:07.225 --> 00:20:09.425
I've spent a fair bit of
time my daughter is now 13.

00:20:09.425 --> 00:20:13.020
I spent a fair bit of time volunteering
in her schools over the years.

00:20:13.020 --> 00:20:17.407
And I think back on is like that's
really impressive communication.

00:20:17.407 --> 00:20:18.802
&gt;&gt; I wholeheartedly agree.

00:20:18.802 --> 00:20:22.352
I think anybody who teaches
anyone where guilt and

00:20:22.352 --> 00:20:28.027
shame doesn't work as a way of helping
moderate behavior it deserves an award.

00:20:28.027 --> 00:20:31.224
And as somebody who studies
communication anxiety and

00:20:31.224 --> 00:20:33.805
has worked a lot on my own and
helped others.

00:20:33.805 --> 00:20:36.565
I have never been more nervous
than when I was in one of my

00:20:36.565 --> 00:20:38.192
son's kindergarten classes.

00:20:38.192 --> 00:20:42.811
And the teacher had to step out to take
a phone call and I had 24 kindergarteners.

00:20:42.811 --> 00:20:45.898
I have never been more nervous
in front of an audience before.

00:20:45.898 --> 00:20:49.834
So Neil let me ask you the third
question what are the first

00:20:49.834 --> 00:20:54.600
three ingredients that go into
a successful communication recipe?

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:58.646
&gt;&gt; So that was a really tough question and
I tried to take your metaphor a little bit

00:20:58.646 --> 00:21:02.712
literally and thought about what is
the best food that has three ingredients.

00:21:02.712 --> 00:21:04.177
And I would say a peanut butter and

00:21:04.177 --> 00:21:07.646
jelly sandwich is maybe I think the best
food that only had three ingredients.

00:21:07.646 --> 00:21:08.270
&gt;&gt; I agree.

00:21:08.270 --> 00:21:13.101
&gt;&gt; I thought about like the peanut butter
would be like one knowing yourself.

00:21:13.101 --> 00:21:17.462
And that's kind of a big theme of our
book, which is to really understand your

00:21:17.462 --> 00:21:21.956
own values, not think of them vaguely,
but to write them down to revisit them,

00:21:21.956 --> 00:21:24.665
to think about them,
to be open to persuasion.

00:21:24.665 --> 00:21:29.448
And to the jelly I would say is empathy,
which is even it's important to both

00:21:29.448 --> 00:21:32.963
know yourself and
understand others at the same time and

00:21:32.963 --> 00:21:36.063
how their values might
be different from yours.

00:21:36.063 --> 00:21:41.038
And then the bread is really what makes
the peanut butter and jelly touch together

00:21:41.038 --> 00:21:46.025
for the combination which is this balance
between being true to your own values.

00:21:46.025 --> 00:21:47.887
Understanding others.

00:21:47.887 --> 00:21:51.322
Persuading them using their
stories not yours/ But

00:21:51.322 --> 00:21:54.319
also being open to persuasion yourself
&gt;&gt; Wow,

00:21:54.319 --> 00:21:58.946
I've never had anybody literally
take the ask of this question and

00:21:58.946 --> 00:22:03.670
turn it into something so the response
PB and J I think is fantastic.

00:22:03.670 --> 00:22:05.310
Well, Ken and Neil, thank you.

00:22:05.310 --> 00:22:08.567
You have provided us with so
many insightful tips and

00:22:08.567 --> 00:22:13.610
guidance that will help us to be better
more ethical leaders and communicators.

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:15.083
Thank you so much.

00:22:15.083 --> 00:22:18.739
And finally, good luck with your new book,
Leading with Values,

00:22:18.739 --> 00:22:22.284
Strategies for Making Ethical Decisions
in Business and Life.

00:22:22.284 --> 00:22:26.545
I personally took a lot of value from it
and I know everybody who reads it will be

00:22:26.545 --> 00:22:29.445
better off and
make better decisions in the future.

00:22:29.445 --> 00:22:30.843
Thank you both.

00:22:30.843 --> 00:22:32.352
&gt;&gt; Thanks for the great conversation.

00:22:32.352 --> 00:22:32.862
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Matt.

00:22:32.862 --> 00:22:35.560
It's been great to have
a chance to talk with you.

